//arxiv.org/html/math/0603293v3\",\"pattern\":\"Theorem 19\"}); Total lines: 1023\nL310: The other possibility which Liu leaves open is that $q=2^{6}$ and $(v,k)=(2080,12)$. In this case a line-stabilizer in $G$ has size $8$ and so contains at most 7 involutions. Applying Lemma 【62†16】 we have\nL311: \nL312:  | $$k\\leq\\frac{r_{g}v}{n_{g}}+1\\leq\\frac{2080\\times 7}{63\\times 65}+1<5.$$  |\nL313: \nL314: This is a contradiction and this possibility can be excluded as required. ∎\nL315: ## 3 Background Information on $PSL(3,q)$\nL316: \nL317: We will sometimes precede the structure of a subgroup of a projective group with $\\hat{\\ }$ which means that we are giving the structure of the pre-image in the corresponding linear group. We will also refer to elements of this linear group in terms of matrices under the standard modular representation.\nL318: ### 3.1 Subgroup information\nL319: \nL320: We need information about the subgroups of $PSL(3,q)$, $PSL(2,q)$ and $GL(2,q)$. Here $q=p^{a}$ for a prime $p$, positive integer $a$.\nL321: ###### Theorem 19.\nL322: \nL323: [【63†14】, 【64†17】, 【65†1】, 【66†12】] The maximal subgroups of $PSL(3,q)$ are among the following list. Conditions given are necessary for existence and maximality but not sufficient. The first three types are all maximal for $q\\geq 5$.\nL324:  | Description  | Notes\nL325: --- | --- | ---\nL326: 1  | $\\hat{\\ }[q^{2}]:GL(2,q)$  | two $PSL(3,q)$-conjugacy classes\nL327: 2  | $\\hat{\\ }(q-1)^{2}:S_{3}$  | one $PSL(3,q)$-conjugacy class\nL328: 3  | $\\hat{\\ }(q^{2}+q+1).3$  | one $PSL(3,q)$-conjugacy class\nL329: 4  | $PSL(3,q_{0}).(q-1,3,b)$  | $q=q_{0}^{b}$ where $b$ is prime\nL330: 5  | $PSU(3,q_{0})$  | $q=q_{0}^{2}$\nL331: 6  | $A_{6}$  |\nL332: 7  | $3^{2}.SL(2,3)$  | $q$ odd\nL333: 8  | $3^{2}.Q_{8}$  | $q$ odd\nL334: 9  | $SO(3,q)\\cong PGL(2,q)$  | $q$ odd\nL335: 10  | $PSL(2,7)$  | $q$ odd\nL336: We will refer to maximal subgroups of $PSL(3,q)$ as being of type $x$, where $x$ is a number between 1 and 10 corresponding to the list above.\nL337: \nL338: Referring to [【65†1】, 【63†14】] we state the following lemma:\nL339: ###### Lemma 20.\nL340: \nL341: Suppose that $H$ is a subgroup of $PSL(3,q)$ lying in a maximal subgroup of type 4 or 5 and $H$ does not lie in any other maximal subgroup of $PSL(3,q)$. Then one of the following holds:\nL342: \nL343:   * •\nL344: \nL345: $H$ has a cyclic normal subgroup of index less than or equal to 3;\nL346: \nL347:   * •\nL348: \nL349: $H$ has socle $PSL(3,q_{1}),PSU(3,q_{1})$ or $PSL(2,q_{1})$ with index less than or equal to 3. Here $q=q_{1}^{c}$, $c$ an integer;\nL350: \nL351:   * •\nL352: \nL353: $H$ is a subgroup of $3^{2}.Q_{8}$ or $3^{2}.SL(2,3)$;\nL354: \nL355:   * •\nL356: $H$ is isomorphic to $A_{5}$ or $PSL(2,7)$;\nL357: \nL358:   * •\nL359: \nL360: $H$ is isomorphic to $A_{6}$ and $q\\equiv 1$ or $19(30)$;\nL361: \nL362:   * •\nL363: \nL364: $H$ is isomorphic to $A_{6}$, $A_{6}.2$ or $A_{7}$, furthermore $q=5^{a},a$ even.\nL365: \nL366: We state a result given by Suzuki [【67†19】, Theorem 6.25] which gives the structure of all the subgroups of $PSL(2,q)$:\nL367: ###### Theorem 21.\nL368: \nL369: Let $q$ be a power of the prime $p$. Let $d=(q-1,2)$. Then a subgroup of $PSL(2,q)$ is isomorphic to one of the following groups.\nL370: \nL371:   1. 1.\nL372: \nL373: The dihedral groups of order $2(q\\pm 1)/d$ and their subgroups.\nL374: \nL375:   2. 2.\nL376: \nL377: A parabolic group $P_{1}$ of order $q(q-1)/d$ and its subgroups. A Sylow $p$-subgroup $P$ of $P_{1}$ is elementary abelian, $P\\lhd P_{1}$ and the factor group $P_{1}/P$ is a cyclic group of order $(q-1)/d$.\nL378: \nL379:   3. 3.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nATLAS: Janko group J1 (https://brauer.maths.qmul.ac.uk/Atlas/v3/spor/J1/)\nciteturn2277view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://brauer.maths.qmul.ac.uk/Atlas/v3/spor/J1/\",\"pattern\":\"